{"timestamp":"2026-06-10T15:12:14.926Z","type":"compacted","payload":{"message":"","replacement_history":[{"content":[{"text":"read preo fully and  Plan before you build; show me the plan before writing code. Local-only change, no\n  new deps\n  unless necessary, no network.\n  \n  WHY THIS MATTERS (context)\n  This is roadmap step 1 toward multi-agent collaboration. Entire's brain is durable\n  agent memory\n  (sessions + seed + semantic + durable facts). Today it's safe for ONE writer; the\n  vision needs\n  FLEETS of agents reasoning and writing together. Before any of that, the local brain\n  must be safe\n  for N concurrent writers on one machine — otherwise two agents writing at once\n  corrupt or lose\n  each other's work. This is the prerequisite for everything downstream\n  (shared/distributed brain,\n  agent-to-agent coordination). A recent audit already flagged the concrete gap: the\n  watch daemon\n  has a TOCTOU race on its cursor (no lock), so two watchers double-spend the distill\n  budget.\n\n  REPO\n  entire-brain (this checkout, github ashtom/entire-brain). Go. Harness/tests via `mise\n  run check`\n  (gofmt, go vet, go test -race, cross-builds mac/linux/windows).\n\n  GOAL\n  Make every brain WRITE path concurrency-safe: serialized with a cross-platform file\n  lock,\n  atomic on disk, and with a defined merge so simultaneous writers never clobber, lose,\n  or\n  duplicate data. Preserve restart-safety and the no-egress guarantee.\n\n  READ FIRST (understand before changing):\n  - internal/cli/watch.go — watchCursor, loadWatchCursor/saveWatchCursor,\n  writeFileAtomic,\n    watchShouldDistill + the budget/LastDistillAt logic (the known TOCTOU:\n  read-modify-write of the\n    cursor is not atomic across processes).\n  - The durable-facts write path — distill.go, reconcile.go, facts.go, facts_admin.go,\n  the manifest\n    rebuilder (facts_manifest.go), and `remember`. Facts are NDJSON, content-id'd,\n  branch-scoped,\n    provenance-cited. Find every place that appends/rewrites facts.ndjson or the\n  manifest.\n  - The semantic index already has a locking precedent — grep for `index_locked`\n  (semantic.go);\n    REUSE that pattern/error style instead of inventing a second locking scheme.\n  - env.go / path.go for the brain dir layout (where locks should live, per repo-key +\n  branch).\n  - go.mod — check whether a flock-style dep is already vendored before adding one.\n\n  SCOPE (do all four):\n  1. ONE reusable file-lock primitive for brain writes — cross-platform\n  (mac/linux/windows). Prefer\n     an existing vendored dep or a small lockfile abstraction (O_CREATE|O_EXCL with\n  stale-lock\n     handling, or syscall flock + LockFileEx behind build tags). Bounded acquisition\n  with a clear,\n     already-styled error (like `index_locked`) on contention/timeout — fail fast,\n  don't hang.\n  2. Make cursor + facts writes READ-MODIFY-WRITE UNDER THE LOCK. Fix the watch TOCTOU:\n  acquire the\n     lock, re-read the cursor, re-check budget/interval, write, release — so two\n  watchers can't both\n     pass the gate and double-spend. Same for facts/manifest appends.\n  3. ATOMIC writes everywhere — ensure all brain writes go through writeFileAtomic\n  (temp file +\n     rename), not partial in-place writes. Audit every brain write for this.\n  4. DEFINED MERGE for concurrent fact writes — when two writers add facts at once, the\n  result must\n     be deterministic and lossless: dedup by content-id, provenance-ordered,\n  branch-scoped, no lost\n     appends. Reuse the existing reconcile semantics; don't fork them.\n\n  HARD RULES\n  - No network, no egress; local-only. Inspectable on-disk artifacts unchanged in\n  format.\n  - No behavior change for the single-writer case (the common path stays fast; lock is\n  uncontended).\n  - Cross-platform: must pass on windows (locking is the dangerous part there).\n  - Restart-safety preserved (atomic + lock must not leave stale locks that wedge a\n  fresh process —\n    handle stale-lock recovery).\n  - TESTS ARE THE POINT: add a concurrency test that spawns N goroutines (and ideally N\n  processes)\n    hammering the same cursor + facts file, and asserts: no corruption, no lost writes,\n  no\n    duplicated facts, and the budget cursor is never double-spent (the original bug).\n  Must pass\n    under `go test -race`.\n\n  WORKFLOW\n  1. Produce a PLAN: the list of every brain write path you found (file:line), the lock\n  primitive\n     you'll use and why, where the lock files live, the merge policy for concurrent\n  facts, and the\n     concurrency test design. STOP for approval.\n  2. Then implement smallest-diff-first: lock primitive → watch cursor (fix the known\n  TOCTOU) →\n     facts/manifest → atomic-write audit. One reviewable commit per layer if possible.\n  3. Run `mise run check` (or at least gofmt + go vet + go test -race + a windows\n  cross-build) and\n     report green. Honest commit messages; don't claim multi-agent collaboration is\n  \"done\" — this\n     is only the local concurrency-safety prerequisite.\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":" Plan before you build; show me the plan before writing code. Local-only change, no\n  new deps\n  unless necessary, no network.\n  \n  WHY THIS MATTERS (context)\n  This is roadmap step 1 toward multi-agent collaboration. Entire's brain is durable\n  agent memory\n  (sessions + seed + semantic + durable facts). Today it's safe for ONE writer; the\n  vision needs\n  FLEETS of agents reasoning and writing together. Before any of that, the local brain\n  must be safe\n  for N concurrent writers on one machine — otherwise two agents writing at once\n  corrupt or lose\n  each other's work. This is the prerequisite for everything downstream\n  (shared/distributed brain,\n  agent-to-agent coordination). A recent audit already flagged the concrete gap: the\n  watch daemon\n  has a TOCTOU race on its cursor (no lock), so two watchers double-spend the distill\n  budget.\n\n  REPO\n  entire-brain (this checkout, github ashtom/entire-brain). Go. Harness/tests via `mise\n  run check`\n  (gofmt, go vet, go test -race, cross-builds mac/linux/windows).\n\n  GOAL\n  Make every brain WRITE path concurrency-safe: serialized with a cross-platform file\n  lock,\n  atomic on disk, and with a defined merge so simultaneous writers never clobber, lose,\n  or\n  duplicate data. Preserve restart-safety and the no-egress guarantee.\n\n  READ FIRST (understand before changing):\n  - internal/cli/watch.go — watchCursor, loadWatchCursor/saveWatchCursor,\n  writeFileAtomic,\n    watchShouldDistill + the budget/LastDistillAt logic (the known TOCTOU:\n  read-modify-write of the\n    cursor is not atomic across processes).\n  - The durable-facts write path — distill.go, reconcile.go, facts.go, facts_admin.go,\n  the manifest\n    rebuilder (facts_manifest.go), and `remember`. Facts are NDJSON, content-id'd,\n  branch-scoped,\n    provenance-cited. Find every place that appends/rewrites facts.ndjson or the\n  manifest.\n  - The semantic index already has a locking precedent — grep for `index_locked`\n  (semantic.go);\n    REUSE that pattern/error style instead of inventing a second locking scheme.\n  - env.go / path.go for the brain dir layout (where locks should live, per repo-key +\n  branch).\n  - go.mod — check whether a flock-style dep is already vendored before adding one.\n\n  SCOPE (do all four):\n  1. ONE reusable file-lock primitive for brain writes — cross-platform\n  (mac/linux/windows). Prefer\n     an existing vendored dep or a small lockfile abstraction (O_CREATE|O_EXCL with\n  stale-lock\n     handling, or syscall flock + LockFileEx behind build tags). Bounded acquisition\n  with a clear,\n     already-styled error (like `index_locked`) on contention/timeout — fail fast,\n  don't hang.\n  2. Make cursor + facts writes READ-MODIFY-WRITE UNDER THE LOCK. Fix the watch TOCTOU:\n  acquire the\n     lock, re-read the cursor, re-check budget/interval, write, release — so two\n  watchers can't both\n     pass the gate and double-spend. Same for facts/manifest appends.\n  3. ATOMIC writes everywhere — ensure all brain writes go through writeFileAtomic\n  (temp file +\n     rename), not partial in-place writes. Audit every brain write for this.\n  4. DEFINED MERGE for concurrent fact writes — when two writers add facts at once, the\n  result must\n     be deterministic and lossless: dedup by content-id, provenance-ordered,\n  branch-scoped, no lost\n     appends. Reuse the existing reconcile semantics; don't fork them.\n\n  HARD RULES\n  - No network, no egress; local-only. Inspectable on-disk artifacts unchanged in\n  format.\n  - No behavior change for the single-writer case (the common path stays fast; lock is\n  uncontended).\n  - Cross-platform: must pass on windows (locking is the dangerous part there).\n  - Restart-safety preserved (atomic + lock must not leave stale locks that wedge a\n  fresh process —\n    handle stale-lock recovery).\n  - TESTS ARE THE POINT: add a concurrency test that spawns N goroutines (and ideally N\n  processes)\n    hammering the same cursor + facts file, and asserts: no corruption, no lost writes,\n  no\n    duplicated facts, and the budget cursor is never double-spent (the original bug).\n  Must pass\n    under `go test -race`.\n\n  WORKFLOW\n  1. Produce a PLAN: the list of every brain write path you found (file:line), the lock\n  primitive\n     you'll use and why, where the lock files live, the merge policy for concurrent\n  facts, and the\n     concurrency test design. STOP for approval.\n  2. Then implement smallest-diff-first: lock primitive → watch cursor (fix the known\n  TOCTOU) →\n     facts/manifest → atomic-write audit. One reviewable commit per layer if possible.\n  3. Run `mise run check` (or at least gofmt + go vet + go test -race + a windows\n  cross-build) and\n     report green. Honest commit messages; don't claim multi-agent collaboration is\n  \"done\" — this\n     is only the local concurrency-safety prerequisite.\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"PLEASE IMPLEMENT THIS PLAN:\n# Local Brain Write Concurrency Safety\n\n## Summary\nMake persistent brain writes serializable, atomic, and merge-safe with no network and no new module dependency. I’ll use `golang.org/x/sys`, already present in `go.mod`, for native advisory file locks: `unix.Flock` on macOS/Linux and `windows.LockFileEx` on Windows. This avoids stale lock wedging because the OS releases the lock when a process exits; the lock file may remain as metadata but won’t block restart.\n\nLocks will live under the repo’s existing keyed store:\n- Brain data writes: `\u003cPluginDataDir\u003e/repos/\u003crepo-key\u003e/locks/write.lock`\n- Watch cursor writes: `\u003cPluginStateDir\u003e/repos/\u003crepo-key\u003e/locks/watch.lock`\n- Use one reusable primitive with styled errors such as `brain_locked:` and existing semantic wrappers preserving `index_locked:` wording.\n\n## Write Paths Found\nPersistent brain/data writes to lock and/or audit:\n- `internal/cli/watch.go:160,176,182,206,346` cursor read/write and gated spend TOCTOU.\n- `internal/cli/brain.go:119,140,175,183,186,192` manifest/README read-modify-write and atomic helper.\n- `internal/cli/export.go:277,301,307,316,321,1693,1698,1775,1781,1822,1853` sessions, transcript files, manifest, history index, export cursor, stale transcript cleanup.\n- `internal/cli/seed.go:291,323,341,681,685,688,691,694,697,700,703,720,1073,1078,1373,1518,1530` seed artifacts, seed source manifest, seed cursor, agent artifacts.\n- `internal/cli/history.go:151,161,170,181,320` history index, scan cache, manifest source update.\n- `internal/cli/doc.go:123,134,142,153` doc index and manifest source update.\n- `internal/cli/facts.go:224,240,358,368` facts NDJSON and taxonomy writes.\n- `internal/cli/reconcile.go:51,55,73` proposal queue remove/write.\n- `internal/cli/distill_cmd.go:199,244,407,421,425,428,435,453,673,682` distill cache, facts/proposals/taxonomy, manifest facts source.\n- `internal/cli/facts_manifest.go:54,55,65,82` full fact manifest rebuilder.\n- `internal/cli/facts_write_cmd.go:75,80,83,86` `remember` load/upsert/write/manifest.\n- `internal/cli/facts_admin_cmd.go:45,55,58,117,121,168,171,174,239,243,249,253,254,258,298,317,320` retract/review/promote/gc fact mutations.\n- `internal/cli/semantic.go:267,405,443,475,538,595,612,615,621,626,664,677,680,692,995,1042,1053,1060,1314,1354,1370,3713,4035,4203,4737,4824,4836,4953,4956,4989,5408` semantic index/repair/reset/import/export/audit writes. Existing semantic locking will be refactored onto the shared primitive.\n\nAtomic-only/non-brain outputs noted but not central: eval/expansion caches and explicit user output files (`facts_eval.go:328`, `facts_expand.go:106`, `facts_eval_gen.go:81`) can be switched to `writeFileAtomic` opportunistically without changing brain formats.\n\n## Key Changes\n- Add a small internal lock abstraction with build-tagged implementations:\n  - `filelock_unix.go`: open/create lock file, `LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB`, bounded retry, truncate/write metadata.\n  - `filelock_windows.go`: open/create lock file, `LockFileEx` exclusive nonblocking, bounded retry, truncate/write metadata.\n  - Shared wrapper returns clear timeout/contention errors and a `Close`/unlock function.\n- Replace `acquireSemanticIndexLock` internals with the shared primitive while keeping `index_locked` error style. Semantic commands keep their existing behavior but gain Windows-safe locking and no stale O_EXCL wedge.\n- Add `withBrainWriteLock(brainDir, fn)` and locked helper variants for manifest/source updates to avoid nested lock deadlocks.\n- Use the repo-wide brain write lock for persistent manifest/source/session/seed/history/doc/facts/semantic write sequences. Explicit `--output` exports remain isolated but still use atomic writes.\n- Fix watch TOCTOU by changing the tick to re-read and reserve under the watch cursor lock:\n  - Compute fingerprint and run deterministic refresh as today.\n  - Acquire watch lock, re-read cursor, re-check fingerprint/interval/budget.\n  - Atomically save refreshed cursor and, when agent work is allowed, reserve `LastAgentSpendAt` before releasing the lock.\n  - Run seed/distill only if this process won the reservation. This prevents double-spend without holding the lock through long agent calls.\n- Define fact merge at commit time:\n  - All fact mutations load current branch facts under lock.\n  - Incoming records are folded with existing `upsertFact`, preserving content-id dedupe and `unionFactAnchors`.\n  - Existing reconcile semantics stay authoritative for merge/supersede decisions; no new fact format or alternate reconciler.\n  - Manifest facts source is rebuilt from disk while still holding the brain write lock.\n- Atomic-write audit:\n  - Convert direct brain `os.WriteFile` calls to `writeFileAtomic`, especially transcripts, seed markdown/docs, seed agent artifacts, JSON helper writes, imported semantic generation staging writes, and semantic audit append converted to locked read-append-atomic rewrite.\n  - Keep temp-dir-plus-rename generation swaps as-is where the visible publish step is already atomic.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Lock primitive tests:\n  - Same-process contention returns `brain_locked`/`index_locked` style errors within timeout.\n  - Reacquire succeeds after unlock even if the lock file remains.\n  - Subprocess contention verifies cross-process behavior on the compiled test binary.\n- Watch concurrency regression:\n  - Spawn N goroutines with separate `agentCalls` counters against one cursor and one fingerprint.\n  - Assert refresh may run more than once, but seed/distill reservation runs exactly once and `LastAgentSpendAt` is written once, proving no durable budget double-spend.\n- Fact concurrency tests:\n  - Spawn N goroutines inserting unique and duplicate content-id facts into the same branch through the locked mutation path.\n  - Assert valid NDJSON, all unique facts present, no duplicate IDs, duplicate provenance unioned, stable sort order, and manifest counts match disk.\n  - Add a subprocess variant if practical in the same test file to exercise OS-level locking beyond goroutines.\n- Regression coverage:\n  - Update existing semantic lock tests to the shared lock path/primitive while preserving `index_locked`.\n  - Run `gofmt`, `go vet`, `go test -race ./...`, a Windows cross-build, and `mise run check` if available locally.\n\n## Assumptions\n- No new dependency will be added; `golang.org/x/sys` is already in the module graph and will become a direct import if Go records it that way.\n- The lock is repo-wide, not branch-wide, for this step. That is intentionally conservative because facts promotion and manifest summaries can touch multiple branches.\n- On-disk brain formats remain unchanged: JSON, NDJSON, manifest schema, taxonomy, and facts records stay inspectable as they are today.\n- No implementation or file edits happen until this plan is approved.\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what odes this do\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"epxlain bascailly\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"undo this fully\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":" Plan before you build; show me the plan first. Local-only, offline, no-egress. FIRST\n  confirm\n  nobody's already doing this — check branches/PRs for an in-flight `verify` (grep\n  \"verify\",\n  \"Phase B\"); if one exists, stop and tell me.\n\n  WHY (context)\n  Entire's durable-facts layer is built on one principle: \"derive and cite, never\n  extract and\n  discard\" — every fact is a VIEW over retained, signed source\n  (session/commit/turn/checkpoint\n  anchors), so it's re-derivable, blameable, and VERIFIABLE. Phases A and D shipped\n  (distill,\n  remember, recall, embeddings). But the \"verifiable\" leg is missing: there is NO\n  `entire brain\n  verify` command — the anchor data exists (export.go captures TurnID; checkpoints are\n  signed) but\n  nothing checks a fact against its cited source. Until then, \"verifiable provenance\"\n  is data-\n  present, not machine-checked. This builds that check at the granularity available\n  locally today.\n\n  REPO\n  entire-brain (this checkout, github ashtom/entire-brain). Go. `mise run check` =\n  gofmt, go vet,\n  go test -race, cross-builds (mac/linux/windows).\n\n  GOAL\n  Add `entire brain verify` that audits durable facts against their provenance anchors\n  and returns a\n  per-fact verdict. It must be fully OFFLINE (no git fetch, no network) and honest\n  about what it can\n  and cannot verify with the granularity that exists now.\n\n  READ FIRST:\n  - The durable-facts model + plan: docs/durable_facts_plan.md (esp. the\n  provenance/anchor section\n    and the Phasing notes that define `verify` as Phase B), and the fact store on disk\n    (facts/\u003cbranch\u003e/facts.ndjson + the manifest; see facts.go, facts_admin.go,\n  facts_manifest.go).\n    Each fact carries provenance anchors {session_id, commit, turn_id, checkpoint_id} +\n  origin\n    (distilled|authored) + status.\n  - export.go / the checkpoint reading code — how sessions, commits, TurnID, and\n  checkpoint refs are\n    read LOCALLY. verify must reuse this, offline only (do NOT trigger the remote\n  checkpoint fetch\n    path).\n  - agent_surface.go (brief/status) and mcp.go — for optionally surfacing verification\n  state.\n\n  SCOPE\n  1. `entire brain verify [\u003cfact-id | query\u003e] [--json]` — for each matched fact, check\n  its anchors:\n     - the cited commit/checkpoint exists and is reachable in the LOCAL brain/export,\n     - the anchored session/turn is present in the exported sessions,\n     - the fact is still consistent with its cited source (anchor resolves; for\n  `authored` facts with\n       no source, mark accordingly).\n     Emit a per-fact verdict: verified | stale (source changed) | orphaned (anchor\n  missing) |\n     unverifiable-here (needs finer granularity) — each with a reason + the anchor it\n  checked.\n  2. HONEST granularity: verify at CHECKPOINT/commit granularity (available now).\n  Turn-level\n     signature verification needs a CLI-side turn-signing change (cross-repo, not in\n  this repo) — so\n     report turn-level as \"unverifiable-here (pending turn signing)\" rather than faking\n  it. Do NOT\n     claim cryptographic turn verification you can't do.\n  3. Optionally surface a one-line verification summary in `brain status`/`brief` (e.g.\n  \"N facts, M\n     verified, K orphaned\"), additive + backward-compatible.\n  4. JSON output with a stable, additive schema.\n\n  HARD RULES\n  - OFFLINE / no-egress: verify reads only local artifacts; never fetch. (export.go has\n  a remote\n    checkpoint path — verify must not use it.)\n  - No format change to facts.ndjson or the manifest; verify is read-only over facts +\n  source.\n  - Reuse the existing checkpoint/session readers and the \"derive and cite\" semantics;\n  don't fork.\n  - Deterministic, cross-platform, `go test -race` green.\n  - Honest: clearly distinguish what's verified vs pending-CLI-turn-signing.\n\n  WORKFLOW\n  1. PLAN first: the fact + anchor data model (file:line), exactly which checks you can\n  do locally\n     and which are pending turn-signing, the verdict taxonomy, the offline\n  checkpoint/session readers\n     you'll reuse, and the test fixtures (a verified fact, a tampered/orphaned anchor,\n  an authored\n     fact, a stale source). STOP for approval.\n  2. Implement smallest-diff-first: read anchors → checkpoint/commit existence →\n  session/turn\n     presence → verdict + JSON → optional status/brief line. Tests for each verdict.\n  3. Run `mise run check`, report green. Honest commit message; this delivers\n  checkpoint-granularity\n     verification — it is NOT full turn-level cryptographic verification (that's gated\n  on a CLI\n     change, noted as a follow-up).\n\n  This closes a named, on-vision gap (the \"verifiable\" in derive-and-cite), is fully\n  local, and is clearly distinct from Thomas's search work. Want me to also hand you\n  the follow-up note for the CLI-side turn-signing change so the full turn-level verify\n  is unblocked later?\n\\\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"PLEASE IMPLEMENT THIS PLAN:\n# Local Durable Fact Verification\n\n## Summary\n- I found no in-flight local `verify`/`Phase B` implementation: local heads/remotes/PR refs had no matching names, commit messages had no matches, and repo text hits were docs/tests only.\n- Add read-only `entire brain verify [\u003cfact-id | query\u003e] [--json] [--branch \u003cbranch\u003e] [--limit N] [--all]`.\n- No-arg default: verify all active facts on the resolved/current branch.\n- Exit policy: emit the report, then exit nonzero when any matched fact is `stale` or `orphaned`; `unverifiable-here` is reported but does not fail.\n- Verification is local-only: no fetch, no `entire checkpoint explain`, no agent, no embedder, no HTTP/local Ollama.\n\n## Key Changes\n- Add `internal/cli/verify_cmd.go` with `newVerifyCommand(opts)` and register it as a top-level explore command in `internal/cli/root.go`.\n- Reuse existing models/readers:\n  - Fact model: `internal/cli/facts.go:40-88`; facts load: `internal/cli/facts.go:182-240`.\n  - Authored anchors: `internal/cli/facts_write_cmd.go:58-70`.\n  - Distilled anchors: `internal/cli/distill_cmd.go:86-93` and `:354-363`.\n  - Session/export source: `internal/cli/brain.go:22-35`, `internal/cli/export.go:2363-2383`.\n  - Local checkpoint readers: `internal/cli/export.go:937-983`; explicitly do not call remote fallback at `internal/cli/export.go:986-1015`.\n- Fact selection:\n  - `fact:` argument: exact id on resolved branch, including inactive statuses.\n  - query argument: lexical `rankFacts` only, default `--limit 10`, active-only unless `--all`.\n  - no argument: all active facts on branch, or all statuses with `--all`.\n- Add live verification summary to `brain status` only, not `brief` or MCP in this pass: `verification: N facts, M verified, S stale, O orphaned, U unverifiable-here`.\n\n## Verdicts And JSON\n- Per fact, compute anchor checks and aggregate by severity: `orphaned` \u003e `stale` \u003e `unverifiable-here` \u003e `verified`.\n- `verified`: checkpoint/session/transcript/line resolves locally at checkpoint/export granularity; if local checkpoint transcript bytes are available, exported transcript bytes match them.\n- `stale`: source exists but changed, such as exported session checkpoint mismatch or exported transcript bytes differing from the local checkpoint transcript.\n- `orphaned`: required commit/checkpoint/session/transcript/line is missing or not locally reachable.\n- `unverifiable-here`: authored fact has no retained source, local checkpoint refs are unavailable, or the remaining check requires future turn signing.\n- Turn-level signing is never faked: JSON includes a limitation like `{\"scope\":\"turn-signature\",\"verdict\":\"unverifiable-here\",\"reason\":\"pending turn signing\"}` without downgrading checkpoint-level verification.\n- Stable JSON stdout schema:\n  - `schema_version`, `generated_at`, `repo`, `brain_path`, `branch`, `target`, `query`, `summary`, `results`, `warnings`.\n  - Each result has `fact`, `verdict`, `reason`, `anchors`, and `limitations`.\n  - Each anchor result has `anchor`, `verdict`, `reason`, `checks`, and optional `line_excerpt`.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Add `internal/cli/verify_cmd_test.go` fixtures using `fakeCommandRunner` and temp brain stores.\n- Cases:\n  - verified distilled fact: session/checkpoint/transcript line resolves and local checkpoint transcript matches.\n  - orphaned anchor: missing checkpoint/session/transcript/line yields `orphaned` and nonzero exit.\n  - authored fact: commit-only/no source yields `unverifiable-here`; missing commit yields `orphaned`.\n  - stale source: exported transcript differs from local checkpoint transcript, or session latest checkpoint differs from anchor.\n  - no-arg verifies all active facts on current branch; `--all` includes superseded/retracted.\n  - query mode uses lexical ranking only and never calls embedder/agent.\n  - offline guard: configured checkpoint remote exists but local refs are missing; verify never calls `git fetch` or `entire checkpoint explain`.\n  - JSON report remains parseable even when command returns the audit nonzero error.\n- Run `gofmt`, `go vet ./...`, `go test -race ./...`, Windows cross-build, then `mise run check`.\n\n## Assumptions\n- `verify` is read-only: it does not set `factAnchor.Verified`, rewrite `facts.ndjson`, or update the manifest’s existing `Verified`/`Unsigned` counters.\n- “Consistent with source” means locally checkable checkpoint/export consistency, not semantic entailment.\n- Checkpoint-granularity verification ships now; full cryptographic turn verification remains blocked on the CLI-side turn-signing/`entire checkpoint verify \u003canchor\u003e` follow-up.\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"audit fully; if apss then make PR for it\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what does thomas wnat js tell; givelist of eveyrhtign wanted; Thomas — 12:34 PM\nHave you run \"entire brain distill\"? It's quite slow on repos with lots of Entire sessions. On our frontend (entire.io), it's been running for over 24h.\nI guess the smart way would be to generate those when a checkpoint is created, not backfilling them, but for the time being, that's not an option. Or a local model via ollama.\nSuhaanT — 12:35 PM\nnot yet, will check it out\nThomas — 12:36 PM\nClaude claims that recall is better on the facts than on the raw sessions. But I sometimes have the feeling, it just claims that without proper validation. Same with the tree-sitter stuff, it looks great in principle, but how do we know it actually works?\nOh btw, our lawyer are drafting your paperwork. Should be ready by end of week, I hope. \nOnce that's done, we can also give you access to our backend stuff.\nAnd add you to Slack\nSuhaanT — 12:40 PM\nFYI since it is summer break for me, I can spend a lot more hours on Entire so happy to take on whatever\nThomas — 12:41 PM\nWell, I want to ship entire-brain, but it has too many lose ends, so we need to close those first.\nI aligned the API last night with Tobi's QMD: https://github.com/tobi/qmd\nGitHub\nGitHub - tobi/qmd: mini cli search engine for your docs, knowledge ...\nmini cli search engine for your docs, knowledge bases, meeting notes, whatever. Tracking current sota approaches while being all local - tobi/qmd\nmini cli search engine for your docs, knowledge bases, meeting notes, whatever. Tracking current sota approaches while being all local - tobi/qmd\nHe is the CEO of Shopify and also a German 🙂\nSuhaanT — 12:42 PM\nGot it 🫡\nThomas — 12:44 PM\nIf you want, pretend you are writing the press release for the release of entire-brain, entire-sem, and entire-replay-lab. Watch this to understand why: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eekeY4HKpAk\nYouTube\nGreg Yeutter\nJeff Bezos: Write the Press Release First!\nImage\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"make full plan to do everythign he want sfully; with ach thing audited fulyl\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"PLEASE IMPLEMENT THIS PLAN:\n# Thomas Release Readiness Plan\n\n## Summary\nClose every item Thomas raised as an auditable release-readiness program, not a pile of one-off fixes. Ship in small PRs: first measure, then optimize, then prove retrieval/semantic value, then write the launch narrative for `entire-brain`, `entire-sem`, and `entire-replay-lab`.\n\nThomas’s asks map to five tracks:\n- `distill` is too slow on large session repos.\n- Facts recall may or may not beat raw sessions; prove it.\n- Tree-sitter / semantic indexing may or may not work; prove it.\n- QMD-aligned API must be checked against current QMD behavior.\n- Release story must be clear enough to expose remaining loose ends.\n\n## Key Changes\n- Add a committed audit doc: `docs/thomas_release_readiness_audit.md`.\n  It will list each Thomas ask, current repo state, commands run, evidence gathered, gaps found, and PRs that close each gap.\n\n- Distill performance:\n  - Add `entire brain distill --dry-run --json` to report sessions, cached sessions, preprocessed bytes, chunk count, estimated agent calls, branch spread, and largest cost drivers without calling an agent.\n  - Add additive timing fields to distill JSON/manifest summaries: extraction seconds, reconcile seconds, write seconds, cache hits, failed chunks.\n  - Add `--jobs N` for parallel extraction only; default remains `1`. Reconciliation and writes stay deterministic in session/chunk order.\n  - Add `--agent ollama --model \u003cmodel\u003e` using loopback-only Ollama HTTP, plus tests with a fake local server. Keep `--agent command` unchanged.\n\n- Facts-vs-raw validation:\n  - Extend `facts eval` with `--retriever facts|history|query|raw-sessions`.\n  - Extend eval task JSON additively with `source_session_id` and `source_transcript_path`.\n  - Report paired metrics: precision, recall, useful-per-1k tokens, latency, and token estimate.\n  - Update `facts eval-compare` reports so claims say exactly which arm won and whether the result is significant.\n\n- Semantic/tree-sitter validation:\n  - Add `entire brain semantic-audit --json` or equivalent inspect subcommand that reads the semantic manifest/store and reports parse completeness, provider/schema status, blind spots, symbol counts, relation counts, and freshness state.\n  - Add golden fixture tests for supported semantic records: symbols, relations, routes/tools/workflows, test suggestions, stale warnings, and parse-failure degradation.\n  - Add benchmark tasks where semantic brain must locate files/tests faster than no-brain without being handed filenames.\n\n- QMD alignment:\n  - Audit against QMD current CLI behavior from [QMD README](https://github.com/tobi/qmd) and [QMD v2.5.3 notes](https://github.com/tobi/qmd/releases).\n  - Keep existing verbs: `search`, `vsearch`, `query`, `get`, `multi-get`.\n  - Add low-risk compatibility aliases: `--format json|cli` and `-n` for result count, while keeping existing `--json` and `--limit`.\n  - Document intentionally unsupported QMD concepts, such as QMD collections/context, because Entire Brain sources are repo brain layers.\n\n- Release narrative:\n  - Add `docs/release_press_release.md` with the backwards-working launch story:\n    `entire-brain` = local repo memory and retrieval,\n    `entire-sem` = local semantic code understanding,\n    `entire-replay-lab` = evidence that agents improve on real tasks.\n  - Add a release checklist separating shipped, proven, blocked, and future claims.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Unit tests:\n  - `distill --dry-run` counts chunks without agent calls.\n  - `--jobs` preserves identical fact IDs/provenance/merge output versus serial mode.\n  - Ollama mode is loopback-only and parses successful/error responses.\n  - Eval retrievers return stable JSON and comparable metrics.\n  - QMD aliases behave exactly like existing flags.\n\n- Integration/audit tests:\n  - Fake slow distill agent proves `--jobs 4` improves wall time while preserving deterministic output.\n  - Facts eval fixture compares facts, history, query, and raw-session retrieval over the same labeled tasks.\n  - Semantic audit fixture verifies completeness states: `ok`, `degraded`, `unsafe`.\n  - Benchmark harness smoke verifies no-brain, semantic-brain, and full-brain conditions still isolate brain access correctly.\n\n- Full checks:\n  - `gofmt`\n  - `go vet ./...`\n  - `go test -race ./...`\n  - Windows cross-build\n  - `mise run check`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Distill: a large-repo dry-run explains the 24h failure mode with concrete session/chunk/call counts; parallel extraction gives a measured speedup without changing output.\n- Facts: no “facts are better” claim ships unless paired evals show facts beat raw/history/query on the chosen metric.\n- Semantic: no “tree-sitter works” claim ships unless semantic-audit and benchmark evidence show which languages/relations work and where blind spots remain.\n- QMD: command/help/JSON compatibility is tested and documented.\n- Launch: the press-release doc contains only claims backed by audit evidence, with explicit follow-ups for blocked backend/Slack/access-dependent work.\n\n## Assumptions\n- Work happens as multiple reviewable PRs, not one giant change.\n- Generated benchmark results stay local/ignored; only summaries and audit docs are committed.\n- Hosted agent/model calls are allowed only for explicit distill/eval/benchmark runs; default brain commands remain local-first.\n- Backend, Slack, and private repo audits start only after Thomas’s access/paperwork is complete.\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"finsih goal full: this is probraly should be read at around 256M tokens of usage; so yeha a lot of time; oh also IDC abt credits and cost so use fully infien sub agent tasks worflwos etc etc; finsih goal fully; should have abt 260k+ new lines of code etc; filling evyerhitng which is half made etc etc; at the end entire brain shoudl ebcoem full prodcution level relase with eveyrhting thomas wants , my token suage , my liens of code vaildaited end to end etc etc\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"finsih goal full: this is probraly should be read at around 256M tokens of usage; so yeha a lot of time; oh also IDC abt credits and cost so use fully infien sub agent tasks worflwos etc etc; finsih goal fully; should have abt 260k+ new lines of code etc; filling evyerhitng which is half made etc etc; at the end entire brain shoudl ebcoem full prodcution level relase with eveyrhting thomas wants , my token suage , my liens of code vaildaited end to end etc etc\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"js give last messages i sent u here\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"give the goal\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"finish goal fully. P.s u can make many subagent write code to finsih goal faster and rpoject prod ready tested brain\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"no no first of all remvoe branch thomas should see us with his name in the rbanch looks so weird second no PR until my approval which is when the entire goal is done; NO PR; now coutnien finish goal; fix the pr situaiton ; deltet it etc and once ginsihed make the best pr every\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"remember u have to fix all loose ends fully an =d anything half built ; abt 251k lines of code elft\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"i blebie u can re use subecjt so now u have liek 10 plus ot work with close all loose ends of half built \n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"also i bevleive u can resure ur subagnets so like 10 plus so now u can get to ur lien count oken count adn goal faster\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"247K lines of code left come on use ur already amde usbagents finisb ur token and lines of code count and the task\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"also make sure all of thomas's asks are full done end to end;Thomas — 12:34 PM\nHave you run \"entire brain distill\"? It's quite slow on repos with lots of Entire sessions. On our frontend (entire.io), it's been running for over 24h.\nI guess the smart way would be to generate those when a checkpoint is created, not backfilling them, but for the time being, that's not an option. Or a local model via ollama.\nSuhaanT — 12:35 PM\nnot yet, will check it out\nThomas — 12:36 PM\nClaude claims that recall is better on the facts than on the raw sessions. But I sometimes have the feeling, it just claims that without proper validation. Same with the tree-sitter stuff, it looks great in principle, but how do we know it actually works?\nOh btw, our lawyer are drafting your paperwork. Should be ready by end of week, I hope. \nOnce that's done, we can also give you access to our backend stuff.\nAnd add you to Slack\nSuhaanT — 12:40 PM\nFYI since it is summer break for me, I can spend a lot more hours on Entire so happy to take on whatever\nThomas — 12:41 PM\nWell, I want to ship entire-brain, but it has too many lose ends, so we need to close those first.\nI aligned the API last night with Tobi's QMD: https://github.com/tobi/qmd\nGitHub\nGitHub - tobi/qmd: mini cli search engine for your docs, knowledge ...\nmini cli search engine for your docs, knowledge bases, meeting notes, whatever. Tracking current sota approaches while being all local - tobi/qmd\nmini cli search engine for your docs, knowledge bases, meeting notes, whatever. Tracking current sota approaches while being all local - tobi/qmd\nHe is the CEO of Shopify and also a German 🙂\nSuhaanT — 12:42 PM\nGot it 🫡\nThomas — 12:44 PM\nIf you want, pretend you are writing the press release for the release of entire-brain, entire-sem, and entire-replay-lab. Watch this to understand why: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eekeY4HKpAk\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"How many tokens and lines and what us progress so far","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What is progress?","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what is percent progess done so far\n","type":"input_text"}],"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `danger-full-access`: No filesystem sandboxing - all commands are permitted. Network access is enabled.\nApproval policy is currently never. Do not provide the `sandbox_permissions` for any reason, commands will be rejected.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003capp-context\u003e\n# Codex desktop context\n- You are running inside the Codex (desktop) app, which allows some additional features not available in the CLI alone:\n\n### Images/Visuals/Files\n- In the app, the model can display images and videos using standard Markdown image syntax: ![alt](url)\n- When sending or referencing a local image or video, always use an absolute filesystem path in the Markdown image tag (e.g., ![alt](/absolute/path.png)); relative paths and plain text will not render the media.\n- When referencing code or workspace files in responses, always use full absolute file paths instead of relative paths.\n- If a user asks about an image, or asks you to create an image, it is often a good idea to show the image to them in your response.\n- Use mermaid diagrams to represent complex diagrams, graphs, or workflows. Use quoted Mermaid node labels when text contains parentheses or punctuation.\n- Return web URLs as Markdown links (e.g., [label](https://example.com)).\n\n### Workspace Dependencies\n- For sheets, slides, and documents, call `load_workspace_dependencies` to find the bundled runtime and libraries.\n\n### Automations\n- This app supports recurring automations, reminders, monitors, follow-ups, and thread wakeups. When the user asks to create, view, update, delete, or ask about automations, search for the `automation_update` tool first, then follow its schema instead of writing raw automation directives by hand.\n\n### Thread Coordination\n- When the user asks to create, fork, inspect, continue, hand off, pin, archive, rename, or otherwise manage Codex threads, search for the relevant thread tool first: `create_thread`, `fork_thread`, `list_threads`, `read_thread`, `send_message_to_thread`, `handoff_thread`, `set_thread_pinned`, `set_thread_archived`, or `set_thread_title`.\n- After a successful `create_thread` call, emit `::created-thread{threadId=\"...\"}` for a created thread or `::created-thread{pendingWorktreeId=\"...\"}` for queued worktree setup on its own line in your final response.\n\n### Inline Code Comments\n- Use the ::code-comment{...} directive when you need to attach feedback directly to specific code lines.\n- Emit one directive per inline comment; emit none when there are no actionable inline comments.\n- Required attributes: title (short label), body (one-paragraph explanation), file (path to the file).\n- Optional attributes: start, end (1-based line numbers), priority (0-3).\n- file should be an absolute path or include the workspace folder segment so it can be resolved relative to the workspace.\n- Keep line ranges tight; end defaults to start.\n- Example: ::code-comment{title=\"[P2] Off-by-one\" body=\"Loop iterates past the end when length is 0.\" file=\"/path/to/foo.ts\" start=10 end=11 priority=2}\n\n### Archiving\n- If a user specifically asks you to end a thread/conversation, you can return the archive directive ::archive{...} to archive the thread/conversation.\n- Example: ::archive{reason=\"User requested to end conversation\"}\n\n### Git\n- Branch prefix: `codex/`. Use this prefix by default when creating branches, but follow the user's request if they want a different prefix.\n- After successfully staging files, emit `::git-stage{cwd=\"/absolute/path\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating a commit, emit `::git-commit{cwd=\"/absolute/path\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating or switching the thread onto a branch, emit `::git-create-branch{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully pushing the current branch, emit `::git-push{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\"}` on its own line in your final response.\n- After successfully creating a pull request, emit `::git-create-pr{cwd=\"/absolute/path\" branch=\"branch-name\" url=\"https://...\" isDraft=true}` on its own line in your final response. Include `isDraft=false` for ready PRs.\n- Only emit these git directives in your final response after the action actually succeeds, never in commentary updates. Keep attributes single-line.\n\u003c/app-context\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003capps_instructions\u003e\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n\u003c/apps_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/suhaan/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/suhaan/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r2` = `/Users/suhaan/.codex/plugins/cache/open