{"timestamp":"2026-06-10T16:46:26.920Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_qdhrw6ETn1dEsxM6eK6Pla1X","output":"{\"status\":{\"completed\":\"No `Makefile` found; the actionable task surface is [mise.toml](/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain/mise.toml:6) plus the release/Radar docs. Also note the worktree is already dirty: [docs/release_readiness_audit.md](/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain/docs/release_readiness_audit.md:488) has `44 -> 47` focused tests. I did not edit it.\\n\\n**Fastest Useful Sequence**\\n\\n1. **Current-code Radar/MCP contract smoke**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p go test ./internal/cli -run '^(TestRegression|TestInspectRegressions|TestMCP|TestWorkspaceRegressions)'\\n   ```\\n   Duration observed: ~2.6s for the retained 47-test regex; this broader regex should still be seconds.  \\n   Proves current branch code still passes the live MCP/Radar tests, not just the retained artifact.\\n\\n2. **Retained MCP/Radar tool evidence**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p mise run radar:evidence\\n   ```\\n   Duration observed: 0.17s, PASS.  \\n   Proves the committed deterministic MCP/Radar artifact is hash-valid and covers 47 required tests: location-only redaction, deletion opt-in, workspace Radar, strict MCP args, QMD MCP retrieval, and safe `tool_result` logging. This is citable tool-contract proof, not agent lift.\\n\\n3. **Thomas/Radar saturation screen**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p mise run radar:screen\\n   ```\\n   Duration observed: 0.25s.  \\n   Proves the Thomas question directly: whether Radar has real no-brain headroom or just saturation/audit gaps. Current result: 26 Radar comparisons, 0 proof-ready, 0 promotable; useful for deciding not to spend the next hour on saturated panels.\\n\\n4. **Retained replay-lab release evidence**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p mise run release:evidence\\n   ```\\n   Duration observed: 0.99s, PASS.  \\n   Proves retained release evidence is audit-clean for current manifest scopes: `history` and generic `mcp`. Current retained lane has 2 suites, 16 records, 4 MCP-verified records, 0 named/completed MCP tool records, so it supports generic MCP/history readiness but not Radar agent-lift.\\n\\n5. **Candidate task/panel preflight**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p python3 benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py check --tasks \\\\\\n     entireio-cli-radar-review-base-file-count.json \\\\\\n     entireio-cli-radar-manual-attribution-deletions.json \\\\\\n     entireio-cli-manual-commit-attribution-base.json \\\\\\n     entireio-cli-transcript-reresolve.json\\n   ```\\n   Duration observed: ~20s, PASS.  \\n   Proves the committed Radar/MCP candidate tasks still fail as expected before agents run. This is high-signal and cheap before spending agent time.\\n\\n6. **Other retained claim gates**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p mise run distill:evidence\\n   /usr/bin/time -p mise run facts:evidence\\n   ```\\n   Durations observed: 0.16s and 0.12s, both PASS.  \\n   These are not Radar/MCP lift proof, but they keep release copy honest: distill has narrow scheduler-speed evidence; facts is explicit `no_release_claim`.\\n\\n7. **Semantic freshness gate, currently expected to fail**\\n   ```sh\\n   /usr/bin/time -p mise run semantic:evidence\\n   ```\\n   Duration observed: 1.08s, FAIL.  \\n   It reports `freshness=unsafe`, `head: stale`, `worktree: dirty-unindexed`. This is useful release-readiness signal, but do not burn time debugging Radar with this unless you are ready to refresh the local semantic index.\\n\\nI would not start with `mise run release:readiness` in a no-edit sidecar pass. It runs [check + all evidence gates](/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain/mise.toml:286), and `check` depends on lint tasks including `go mod tidy` via `lint:gomod`, which can modify `go.mod`/`go.sum`. For this next hour, the sequence above gives the best signal/time without touching files.\"}}"}}
